 "grammar": {
  "title": {
   "ko": "some / any · much / many · a few / a little",
   "en": "some / any · much / many · a few / a little"
  },
  "explain": {
   "ko": "셀 수 있는 명사(apple, egg)와 셀 수 없는 명사(milk, rice)에 따라 양을 나타내는 말이 달라집니다. 긍정문에는 some, 부정문과 의문문에는 any를 씁니다. 셀 수 있는 명사에는 many / a few, 셀 수 없는 명사에는 much / a little을 씁니다. a lot of는 둘 다에 쓸 수 있습니다.",
   "en": "The quantity word depends on whether the noun is countable (apple, egg) or uncountable (milk, rice). Use some in positive sentences and any in negatives and questions. Use many / a few with countable nouns and much / a little with uncountable nouns. 'A lot of' works with both."
  },
  "table": {
   "head": [
    "셀 수 있는 / Countable",
    "셀 수 없는 / Uncountable"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "How many eggs?",
     "How much milk?"
    ],
    [
     "many eggs",
     "much milk"
    ],
    [
     "a few eggs",
     "a little milk"
    ],
    [
     "some eggs",
     "some milk"
    ],
    [
     "a lot of eggs",
     "a lot of milk"
    ]
   ]
  },
  "notes": [
   {
    "ko": "much는 보통 부정문과 의문문에서 씁니다: We don't have much time.",
    "en": "'Much' is mainly used in negatives and questions: We don't have much time."
   },
   {
    "ko": "some은 권유·요청 의문문에도 씁니다: Would you like some tea?",
    "en": "Some is also used in offers and requests: Would you like some tea?"
   },
   {
    "ko": "병·봉지로 세는 말: a bottle of water, a bag of rice.",
    "en": "Containers count uncountable nouns: a bottle of water, a bag of rice."
   }
  ],
